[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Most scanners flag a thousand bugs and prove exactly none of them. This one\'s an AI that breaks into your app for real, writes the working exploit, then patches the hole before a hacker ever finds it. Open source, 28,000 stars, and you can point it at your code in about a'

[00:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'minute. Here\'s the dirty secret of security scanners. You run one and it hands you a thousand red warnings. Possible SQL injection. Possible auth bypass. Possible everything.'

[00:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And almost none of it is real. It\'s the static analysis tax. Endless maybes, no proof, and a backlog nobody trusts enough to actually fix. Strict takes the opposite bet. Instead of'

[00:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'guessing from the sidelines, it spins up a team of autonomous AI agents that act like real attackers. They run your code, poke at your endpoints, and try to actually break in. It\'s open source, Apache licensed, and it\'s pulled in tens'

[00:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of thousands of GitHub stars in under a year. And here\'s the part that matters. When a normal scanner says, "Maybe SQL injection." Strict tries to exploit it. If the payload works, if it can actually'

[01:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'dump your users table, it saves the request, the response, and a working proof of concept. No proof, no finding. So, the report you get isn\'t a pile of maybes. It\'s a short list of holes'

[01:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'someone just walked through. So, does it actually work? Point it at a target and it goes to work in real time. Recon first, mapping the attack surface. Then it starts probing, testing inputs,'

[01:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'chaining requests, watching how the app responds, and when it finds something exploitable, it doesn\'t just flag it. It lands the exploit right there in the terminal and tags it with an OWASP'

[01:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'category and a severity score. Finding the bug is only half the job. Strict can also write the fix. It opens a pull request with the patched code, say swapping a hand-built SQL string for a'

[01:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'safe parameterized query, and you just review and merge. Drop it into your CI, and it\'ll quietly fail the build the moment someone pushes a vulnerable change. Okay, but how is 1 AI doing all of this? It'

[02:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'isn\'t 1. Open the repo, and you find what they call a graph of agents. One agent runs recon, another writes exploits, another handles what happens after the break-in. They run in'

[02:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'parallel, share what they discover, and chain small bugs into bigger ones, exactly how a human red team works. And under it all, a single 30 kilobyte system prompt doing the heavy lifting. And these agents come armed. Stric ships'

[02:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a full offensive toolkit, an intercepting proxy to tamper with live traffic, a real browser to fire cross-site scripting and request forgery attacks, a shell and a sandbox for running custom exploit code. The clever'

[02:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'part? It didn\'t reinvent any of it. It wires together the battle-tested tools the pros already use, and lets the AI drive them. Now, real talk. Because the hype crowd won\'t tell you'

[03:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this. Stric is not a firewall. It doesn\'t sit in front of your app blocking attacks. It finds and fixes holes before you ship. That\'s it. It needs Docker. It needs your own model'

[03:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'key, so every scan burns real tokens. And the maintainers say it plainly, only ever point this at apps you actually own. Want to try it? Three lines.'

[03:28] [scene:talk]
